{"name":"Soldier Green","hex":"#545a2c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soldier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a ground-based army, of any rank, but especially an enlisted member.","Any member of a military, regardless of specialty."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To continue steadfast; to keep striving.","To serve as a soldier."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soldier"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soldier","blocks":[{"text":"A soldier is a person who is a member of an army. A soldier can be a conscripted or volunteer enlisted person, a non-commissioned officer, a warrant officer, or an officer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg/330px-2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soldier","wikidata":"Q4991371"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg/500px-Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg/1280px-Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg/500px-Filipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australian Army soldiers in Victoria, 1941","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFilipino_soldiers_in_Victoria%2C_World_War_II.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/U.S._Army_and_French_Soldiers_Participate_in_Marara_22_7193591.jpg/500px-U.S._Army_and_French_Soldiers_Participate_in_Marara_22_7193591.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/U.S._Army_and_French_Soldiers_Participate_in_Marara_22_7193591.jpg/1280px-U.S._Army_and_French_Soldiers_Participate_in_Marara_22_7193591.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thu